The saved `metadata.normal_functional.tile_size` and `metadata.options.normal_tile_size` must remain the actual value used for that checkpoint; do not rewrite the source metadata to the new value before comparing. The canonical compatibility view drops only those two keys. Recomputing the saved canonical fingerprint instead of comparing its historical stored fingerprint is required for already-written v2 checkpoints, whose stored fingerprint included tile size.

Suggested tests (do not run as part of this review)
====================================================

1. **End-to-end resume across tile widths.** Use the existing tiny `_toy_evaluator` checkpoint-resume fixture: train one accepted update with `normal_tile_size=8`, resume the saved v2 checkpoint with `normal_tile_size=16` (and separately 32), and assert resume succeeds, model/optimizer state restores, the resumed event records old and new tile widths, and the final checkpoint retains the new width. The toy evaluator does not prove production integral equivalence, so also add a small bounded PH-only comparison of the same fixed density/fields at tile widths 8/16/32, checking scalar channel totals and primitive fields within a stated roundoff tolerance without optimizing between evaluations.
2. **Hard-guard regressions.** Build the same v2 fixture, then change exactly one of: basis metadata (including same D/P but altered radius/spacing/grid), PKA1 parameter value, or PH `lmax`; each resume must still fail compatibility. Assert the error identifies an incompatible basis/physics contract. Also retain the existing production-provider checks for parameter/basis/lmax consistency.
3. **Provenance and old-checkpoint migration.** Start from a checkpoint whose stored fingerprint was computed with the pre-change compatibility function. Verify the new loader recomputes the canonical fingerprint from its saved metadata, allows only a tile-width difference, and records both widths plus source checkpoint/update number. Verify a second continuation checkpoint preserves the full provenance history and reports the current tile separately from the original source tile.

No claim is made here that changed tiling preserves bitwise energies or optimizer trajectory. It preserves the defined physical discretization and objective; roundoff-scale differences should be quantified in the bounded equivalence test before production resume uses the option.
